WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
A receptor reserve lets a tissue respond fully with mostly empty receptors.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:11.240
Naloxone can reverse a morphine overdose within minutes without reacting with

3
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.740
morphine. How does a blocker's curve shift reveal its mechanism?

4
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:22.733
Start with a competitive antagonist, like naloxone.

5
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:28.193
It binds reversibly at the agonist's own site and switches nothing on.

6
00:00:29.473 --> 00:00:32.693
One receptor holds agonist or blocker, never both.

7
00:00:33.953 --> 00:00:36.513
Add enough agonist, and it wins the sites back.

8
00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:41.577
On a log dose axis, each step multiplies the dose by ten.

9
00:00:42.897 --> 00:00:48.397
The curve slides right in parallel: at every response level, the dose needed rises

10
00:00:48.397 --> 00:00:49.577
by the same factor.

11
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:52.637
That factor is the dose ratio.

12
00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:58.297
Enough agonist still reaches the full ceiling, so this block is surmountable.

13
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.820
Other blockers are insurmountable: more agonist cannot overcome them.

14
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.680
A non-competitive blocker binds elsewhere on the receptor, or stops a downstream

15
00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.520
step. Even with every agonist site filled, the response hits a lower ceiling.

16
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:22.280
An irreversible blocker bonds covalently to the agonist site, so it cannot be washed

17
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.480
off or outcompeted.

18
00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:27.903
Now test an unknown blocker at one concentration.

19
00:01:29.243 --> 00:01:33.203
The agonist curve slides right in parallel, with the same maximum.

20
00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:39.143
Does that single curve prove the blocker is a reversible competitive antagonist?

21
00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:52.617
No. With a receptor reserve, a small irreversible dose also slides the curve right

22
00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:53.877
before the ceiling drops.

23
00:01:55.197 --> 00:01:57.077
Only higher doses separate them.

24
00:01:58.297 --> 00:02:02.797
To name the mechanism, watch how rising doses reshape the agonist curve.

25
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.260
Heinz Schild made rising blocker doses a test for competition.

26
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.360
Raise the blocker tenfold, twice.

27
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.640
Simple competition at equilibrium might give dose ratios of 2, 11 and 101.

28
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.540
Subtract 1, since no blocker means a ratio of 1.

29
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.780
That leaves 1, 10 and 100.

30
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.240
As logs against log blocker dose, they form a straight line of slope 1.

31
00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:36.843
A different slope, or a curve, warns that an assumption failed.

32
00:02:38.203 --> 00:02:41.863
Equilibrium may not hold, or the tissue may remove agonist.

33
00:02:43.043 --> 00:02:46.883
A blocker binding elsewhere, or a second receptor, can bend it too.

34
00:02:48.223 --> 00:02:51.523
Even slope 1 over a narrow range does not rule those out.

35
00:02:52.517 --> 00:02:54.557
Return to naloxone and morphine.

36
00:02:55.837 --> 00:03:01.037
In a simple preparation at equilibrium, naloxone fills morphine's receptor without

37
00:03:01.037 --> 00:03:04.057
activating it, shifting morphine's curve right.

38
00:03:05.257 --> 00:03:10.017
That reduces the signalling behind an overdose's slowed breathing, without touching

39
00:03:10.017 --> 00:03:17.177
morphine. Yet clinical reversal depends on time, and equilibrium ratios alone do not

40
00:03:17.177 --> 00:03:18.177
predict it.

41
00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:24.447
Across rising doses, a curve's shift and ceiling separate surmountable competition

42
00:03:24.447 --> 00:03:26.007
from insurmountable block.

43
00:03:27.387 --> 00:03:31.367
Next: can two curves in an unknown tissue measure a blocker's grip?
